# `<-` binds a blueprint or an element (Semantics ch. 8, "`<-` has three readings"), and
# `ignore <-` drops an output on purpose; it does not make a line of a function that was
# not applied. The checker refuses `$d <- @Distinct` so; with `ignore` on the left it lets
# the line through, and the back end refuses it ("a building line this back end does not
# lower yet"; its guard for a host without an argument stands behind). Written by Claude
# on the claude-lowering branch; subject to change.
emit Ui.*
use Ui.*

@Row: fn Nat -> @unit
fn @Row n:
    Ui.Label($text=$"row {n}")

@page: @unit
@page:
    ignore <- @Row
    Ui.Label($text=$"x")
